{
  "id": "s02-l06",
  "unit": "s02-consonants",
  "title": "Consonants 6: য র ল শ ষ স হ",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "This lesson collects the rest of the chart: **য র ল** (semivowels), **শ ষ স** (sibilants) and **হ**.\n\n**র vs ব**: র (`ro`) is ব with a **dot below**. Bengali r is a quick tap of the tongue, as in Spanish *pero*: রাত (`rat`, *night*). **য** sounds exactly like জ (`j`): যাব (`jabo`, *I'll go*).\n\n**Three letters, one sound.** শ, ষ and স are normally all `sh`: শিক্ষক (`shikkhok`), মানুষ (`manush`), সকাল (`shokal`). স switches to a real `s` in clusters before t, n, r, l (স্টেশন `steshon`). Learn which one a word uses as part of its spelling.\n\nLook-alike drill: **ব র ধ থ য**. ব = triangle; র = triangle + dot; ধ = open top-left; থ = bottom loop. Put them side by side and compare.",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "l-ya",
      "bn": "য",
      "tr": "jo",
      "en": "j as in 'jam' (same sound as জ)",
      "notes": "Name: ontostho jo (অন্তঃস্থ য). With a dot below it becomes য় (y), taught next lesson."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-ra",
      "bn": "র",
      "tr": "ro",
      "en": "r: a light tap of the tongue (like Spanish 'pero')",
      "notes": "Name: bo-ye shunno ro (ব-য়ে শূন্য র), 'ব with a dot'. Never trill or curl it like English r."
    },
    {
      "id": "l-la",
      "bn": "ল",
      "tr": "lo",
      "en": "l as in 'love'",
      "notes": "Name: lo (ল)."
    },
